C++ Cross Platform through dot net framework
Developing a cross-platform solution using C++ integrated with the .NET framework (specifically .NET Core, .NET 5+, and modern .NET) allows developers to leverage high-performance native code alongside the flexibility of managed code across Windows, macOS, and Linux.
The following sections detail how to achieve this integration based on the provided sources.
1. Fundamental Approaches to Integration
Because managed .NET code cannot call unmanaged C++ code directly, an intermediate layer is required. There are two primary methods:

    Platform Invoke (P/Invoke): This is the standard cross-platform method. It involves creating a C API wrapper around your C++ library. C# then uses the DllImport attribute to load this library and call its functions.
    C++/CLI: This creates a managed C++ wrapper library compiled with the Common Language Runtime (/clr). However, this approach is Windows-only because it depends on the MSVC compiler and specific Microsoft libraries.

2. Implementation Workflow for Cross-Platform C++
To build a system that works on multiple platforms, you should focus on the P/Invoke path:

    C API Wrapper: Since C# can call C functions easily, you must wrap C++ classes and methods into procedural C functions. This layer handles the conversion of types, such as turning a std::string into a C-compatible string pointer.
    Build System (CMake): For Windows, CMake can handle both C++ and C# targets in a single project using Visual Studio generators. For non-Windows platforms, you must use CMake for the C++ parts and the dotnet CLI (with .csproj files) for the C# parts, as CMake’s C# support currently requires Visual Studio on Windows.
    Handling Platform Binaries: You must account for different library formats: .dll (Windows), .so (Linux), and .dylib (macOS). Tools like NativeLibraryManager can help automate detecting the platform and extracting the correct embedded binary at runtime.

3. Data Marshalling and Compatibility
Crossing the boundary between native and managed code requires careful data handling:

    String Encoding: .NET strings are UTF-16, while C++ often uses UTF-8. On Windows, you may need to use specific functions like Marshal.PtrToStringUTF8() to avoid corrupted characters in the console. On macOS and Linux, the ANSI variants often handle UTF-8 correctly without extra configuration.
    Type Sizes: Some types vary by platform. For example, a C++ long is 32-bit on Windows but 64-bit on 64-bit macOS and Linux. C# long is always 64-bit, so using CLong and CULong (available in .NET 6+) is recommended for safe interoperability.

4. Frameworks for Cross-Platform UI
If your C++ library needs a graphical interface across different devices:

    .NET MAUI: Can run on any platform and supports binding native libraries (iOS .a frameworks, Android .so files). For iOS specifically, static libraries are preferred as the Apple Store may reject certain dynamic library configurations.
    Uno Platform: An open-source alternative that allows WinUI and XAML-based code to run on iOS, macOS, Linux, Android, Windows, and WebAssembly.

5. Automation with SWIG
SWIG (Simplified Wrapper and Interface Generator) is a powerful tool that automates the creation of these wrappers. It can take your C++ header files and generate the necessary C# P/Invoke code and C++ wrapper code automatically. It supports complex C++ features like classes, inheritance, templates, and namespaces, significantly reducing the manual work required for cross-platform integration.
